candidates.text:
From the given information, \(\angle RPQ\) is the angle at \(P\) in triangle \(RPQ\), and \(\sin \angle RPQ = \frac{7}{25}\).

Thus

\[
\cos \angle RPQ=\sqrt{1-\sin^2\angle RPQ}
=\sqrt{1-\left(\frac{7}{25}\right)^2}
=\sqrt{1-\frac{49}{625}}
=\sqrt{\frac{576}{625}
}=\frac{24}{25}.
\]

Since points \(S,Q,R\) lie on the same side of \(P\) and \(\angle RPS\) is the same angle as \(\angle RPQ\), we have

\[
\boxed{\frac{24}{25}}.
\]

The points \(S(-2,0)\), \(P(0,0)\), and \(Q(2,0)\) lie on the \(x\)-axis, with \(P\) between \(S\) and \(Q\). The point \(R\) is obtained by rotating \((1.5,0)\) by an angle \(\theta\) whose sine is \(\frac{7}{25}\). Thus \(\theta = \angle RPQ\) is acute, with \(\sin\theta = \frac{7}{25}\) and \(\cos\theta = \sqrt{1 - \left(\frac{7}{25}\right)^2} = \frac{24}{25}\).

The ray \(\overrightarrow{PQ}\) points to the right (positive \(x\)-axis), and \(\overrightarrow{PS}\) points to the left (negative \(x\)-axis). The angle \(\angle RPS\) is the angle between \(\overrightarrow{PR}\) and \(\overrightarrow{PS}\). Since \(\overrightarrow{PR}\) is rotated by \(\theta\) from the positive \(x\)-axis, the angle between \(\overrightarrow{PR}\) and the negative \(x\)-axis is \(180^\circ - \theta\). Its cosine is \(\cos(180^\circ - \theta) = -\cos\theta = -\frac{24}{25}\).

\boxed{-\frac{24}{25}}

