Now, \(210 = 2 \cdot 3 \cdot 5 \cdot 7\), so \(210^2 = 2^2 \cdot 3^2 \cdot 5^2 \cdot 7^2\). For two coprime factors whose product is \(N = 210^2\), each prime power must go entirely to \(x\) or to \(y\). With four distinct primes, there are \(2^4 = 16\) assignments. Exactly half of these yield \(x < y\) (and the other half \(x > y\)); none have \(x = y\) because that would make \(\gcd(x,y) = 210 \neq 1\). Therefore there are \(8\) valid pairs \((x,y)\) with \(x < y\). Each such \(x\) gives a distinct value for \(a = 210x\).

\boxed{8}

