winner_texts.self_consistency:
The polynomial \(x^4 + a x^3 - b x^2 + a x + 1 = 0\) has real roots only if there is a real \(x \neq 0\) such that the equation holds. Dividing by \(x^2\) and setting \(t = x + \frac{1}{x}\) yields the quadratic equation \(t^2 + a t - (b+2) = 0\). For real \(x \neq 0\), we must have \(|t| \ge 2\). The roots of this quadratic always have opposite signs and product \(-(b+2) < 0\). Evaluating the quadratic at \(t = 2\) and \(t = -2\) gives \(f(2) = 2a - b + 2\) and \(f(-2) = 2 - 2a - b\). Since \(a,b \in [0,1]\), we have \(f(2) \ge 1 > 0\), so the positive root is always less than 2. The condition for a root with \(|t| \ge 2\) reduces to \(f(-2) \le 0\), i.e., \(2 - 2a - b \le 0\) or \(2a + b \ge 2\).

Within the unit square \([0,1] \times [0,1]\), the inequality \(2a + b \ge 2\) defines a triangle with vertices \((1,0)\), \((1,1)\), and \((0.5,1)\). The area of this triangle is \(\frac{1}{2} \times 1 \times 0.5 = \frac{1}{4}\).

\boxed{\frac{1}{4}}

